 can put all of  those individual examples in context and  look at compute engines overall  functionality  so by now it should be clear that  compute engine is a product that allows  you to run virtual machines a top of  Google's massive infrastructure so tons  and tons of computing cores of memory of  disk storage and of network globally  distributed and a tooling and work flow  that allows you to easily spin up one to  hundreds to thousands to tens of  thousands of VMs and coordinate their  activity and also intelligently route  traffic to the instances based on your  users needs we talked about virtual  machines there's a range of machine  types so you can go from one CPU to 64  CPUs you can go from roughly half a gig  of ram to four hundred and sixteen  gigabytes of ram and they've got a set  of different combinations of resources  so they've got four pre-configured sets  of recipes that are standard which is a  nice blend or balance between CPU and  memory they've got high CPU recipes  where that's like double the CPU for a  given amount of memory they have the  inverse which is high memory instances  and that's like double the amount of  memory for a given amount of CPU they've  got shared CPUs which are the least  expensive and lowest performing VMs and  then if none of these pre-configured  recipes work for you you can make your  own custom recipes and basically pick  how much CPU and how much memory you  want for every VM that you start up  you're going to need attached storage  and so GC offers standard and SSD  persistent disk standard disks are  rotating magnetic media and SSDs are  obviously really good for random i/o  kind of operations disk options whether  they're SSD or persistent offer you very  high availability and durability your  data is actually spread across multiple  disks so if a particular device goes  down your data does not get lost and you  continue to have access your data is  encrypted at rest so Google  automatically encrypts the data and  manages the keys if you work in an  industry where you're required to manage  your own keys you can also provide your  own keys and manage the encryption that  they optimize your disk performance so  if you get like a 10 terabyte volume  that's actually probably a bunch of  hundred gigabyte volumes and your data  is striped across that so you don't have  to do any software raid configurations  and they have snapshots which allow you  to easily backup disks and make copies  and then transport that backup maybe to  other zones or other regions to spin up  instances virtual machines range from  eight tenths of a cent to $4 per  instance hour you only get charged while  the machine is running and there are a  number of really customer friendly  pricing innovations instances are billed  on a per minute basis so for instance if  you use an instance for 11 minutes even  if it's a for an owl or an hour instance  you're only gonna pay roughly one-sixth  of that for you're only gonna pay for  the 11 minutes you use there is a  10-minute minimum but you don't pay for  the whole hour even if you only use a  fraction of an hour there are sustained  use discounts what this means is if you  use an instance 24 hours a day seven  days a week for the entire month you'll  actually get a 30% discount the more you  use it the bigger the marginal discount  becomes they even go so far as to give  you what are called inferred instances  so this is if you run one instance for  two weeks and you run another instance  for another two weeks they'll actually  stitch those two together and say oh you  ran an instance for four weeks and  they'll give you the discount and they  also have this thing called preemptable  instances which is these are instances  that you can get at a huge discount  they're 80 percent less expensive so you  only pay 20% of the price the Google  reserves the right to claw them back at  any time so what that means is if you  have a computed for instance where you  