 can put all of  those individual examples in context and  look at compute engines overall  functionality  so by now it should be clear that  compute engine is a product that allows  you to run virtual machines a top of  Google's massive infrastructure so tons  and tons of computing cores of memory of  disk storage and of network globally  distributed and a tooling and work flow  that allows you to easily spin up one to  hundreds to thousands to tens of  thousands of VMs and coordinate their  activity and also intelligently route  traffic to the instances based on your  users needs we talked about virtual  machines there's a range of machine  types so you can go from one CPU to 64  CPUs you can go from roughly half a gig  of ram to four hundred and sixteen  gigabytes of ram and they've got a set  of different combinations of resources  so they've got four pre-configured sets  of recipes that are standard which is a  nice blend or balance between CPU and  memory they've got high CPU recipes  where that's like double the CPU for a  given amount of memory they have the  inverse which is high memory instances  and that's like double the amount of  memory for a given amount of CPU they've  got shared CPUs which are the least  expensive and lowest performing VMs and  then if none of these pre-configured  recipes work for you you can make your  own custom recipes and basically pick  how much CPU and how much memory you  want for every VM that you start up  you're going to need attached storage  and so GC offers standard and SSD  persistent disk standard disks are  rotating magnetic media and SSDs are  obviously really good for random i/o  kind of operations disk options whether  they're SSD or persistent offer you very  high availability and durability your  data is actually spread across multiple  disks so if a particular device goes  down your data does not get lost and you  continue to have access your data is  encrypted at rest so Google  automatically encrypts the data and  manages the keys if you work in an  industry where you're required to manage  your own keys you can also provide your  own keys and manage the encryption that  they optimize your disk performance so  if you get like a 10 terabyte volume  that's actually probably a bunch of  hundred gigabyte volumes and your data  is striped across that so you don't have  to do any software raid configurations  and they have snapshots which allow you  to easily backup disks and make copies  and then transport that backup maybe to  other zones or other regions to spin up  instances virtual machines range from  eight tenths of a cent to $4 per  instance hour you only get charged while  the machine is running and there are a  number of really customer friendly  pricing innovations instances are billed  on a per minute basis so for instance if  you use an instance for 11 minutes even  if it's a for an owl or an hour instance  you're only gonna pay roughly one-sixth  of that for you're only gonna pay for  the 11 minutes you use there is a  10-minute minimum but you don't pay for  the whole hour even if you only use a  fraction of an hour there are sustained  use discounts what this means is if you  use an instance 24 hours a day seven  days a week for the entire month you'll  actually get a 30% discount the more you  use it the bigger the marginal discount  becomes they even go so far as to give  you what are called inferred instances  so this is if you run one instance for  two weeks and you run another instance  for another two weeks they'll actually  stitch those two together and say oh you  ran an instance for four weeks and  they'll give you the discount and they  also have this thing called preemptable  instances which is these are instances  that you can get at a huge discount  they're 80 percent less expensive so you  only pay 20% of the price the Google  reserves the right to claw them back at  any time so what that means is if you  have a computed for instance where you  need 10 VMs running but you'd like a  hundred you can pay for 10 regular  instances and 90 preemptable instances  and if for some reason they take back 20  or 30% of those instances you still have  a bunch of instances that we're running  at a vastly reduced cost we showed you  during the demo boot disk images and  there are two types of images there are  public images and these are maintained  by Google or third parties and these are  clean operating system boot images so  Debian poor OS Ubuntu there are also  premium public images so like Red Hat  Enterprise Linux or sequel server on  Windows Server these you have to pay an  additional licensing fee on top of the  vm instance fees because you're using  third-party licensable software  generally what happens is you'll start  with a public image and then you'll  install your own software you'll change  the configuration and then you'll save  that as a custom image and that's what  we did in the example so we use our  custom front-end and our custom back-end  images in order to create instance  templates and then auto scaling instance  groups so that is customizing a public  image you can also use the new Google  via migration service this was just  launched in the last couple weeks and it  allows you to actually migrate live  running VMs from other environments to  GCP with no downtime so check that out  or you can also manually take say AWS  virtual machines export them as raw disk  images and then tweak them and import  them into GCP and then run those VMs now  in Google compute engine we had instance  groups and again an instance group is  simply a collection of machines that you  can manage together as opposed to  individually so these allow you to  easily add or remove instances you can  simply change the size of the instance  group and it can spin up additional  instances gives you self-healing on your  instances so if for some reason an  instance goes down and you have a health  check running the instance group can  determine that that instance is no  longer is healthy it can shut it down  and then bring up a replacement instance  with the same configuration we saw auto  scaling and this can be based on CPU  utilization it can be based on requests  per second if it's HTTP load balancing  or it can be based on custom stack  driver metrics so you can basically see  how the machines are being used and once  they exceed an average utilization you  can add additional instances this also  scales down so if you have five  those transcoding servers and then  there's no more transcoding Tugu after  ten minutes it will start backing off  and shutting down instances we saw a  load balancing and we built an HTTP load  balancer which not only distributes the  load across multiple back-end instance  groups but it intelligently routes  traffic to the nearest or fastest  instance group as long as it has  capacity  there's also TCP and UDP load balancing  options available in GCE and then these  instance groups can be zonal or regional  so a zonal instance group is a set of  instances that are all in the same sort  of fault space that means they're all in  the same building in a data center it's  possible that that zone could go down so  power could go out or a network cable  could go down you can choose to make  those instance groups regional instead  and if you do that your instances are  spread across multiple zones which makes  it less likely that if there's an outage  in one zone that that's going to  interrupt service to your end users you  have a sophisticated range of networking  options available to you in Google  compute engine it automatically creates  a network for you when you create a  project that network has a variety of  subnets and those subnets are all route  it routed for you automatically so it  automatically generates these routes  that allow all of these discrete subnets  to talk to one another you can also  create custom routes as necessary the  firewall rules are super flexible so  basically you can say that instances  with particular tabs allow incoming  traffic whereas instances without those  tags don't allow incoming traffic by  default there are a half-dozen firewall  rules created for you but you can tweak  those and add as many as you want  you have the ability to create VPNs so  that your private IP ranges within your  company are considered to be part of the  GCP private address namespace or the the  routing happens between the two of them  so you can easily work in an environment  that bridges your GCP networks and your  internal networks again we talked about  load balancing and you also have cloud  DNS which is GCP hosted public facing  DNS if you wanted to do DNS on the cloud  in terms of managing all this you saw me  work primarily with the cloud console so  I did everything sort of manually as it  turns out all work that you do in  administering Google compute engine is  done through a standard set of API s and  when I'm using the console the console  is talking to those API s  I could also use a command-line utility  like G cloud or gsutil G cloud is for  working with compute engine resources  gsutil is for working with Google Cloud  storage resources all of these basically  talk to the backend API s there are  third-party utilities or you can write  your own custom scripts or applications  to manage your resources as well so the  question is given all of these  capabilities giving the range of  instance types given the instance groups  and load balancing zone and so forth  what use cases can you tackle and what  could you do with compute engine here's  one example for inspiration frame store  is a company that does 3d renderings and  video renderings and basically they use  compute engine for excess burst  rendering capacity so they have a big  render farm on site but occasionally  they need to have a whole bunch more  cores thrown at a distributed rendering  process and so they work with compute  engine and they can spin up to 15,000  cores at the drop of a hat this actually  allows them to dramatically reduce the  rendering time and that allows them to  do a ton of fast turnarounds so they can  actually be more creative as well as hit  deadlines more easily and they save  money doing it considerable amounts of  money relative to having the same sort  of capacity on site this is a big  example of the little example that we  did with the transcoding on the back end  of our LMS now after today when you  start working  with compute engine you may need to go  find answers to specific questions or  guides on how to perform certain  activities so let me give you a few  hints on where to look for additional  information start with search search is  your best friend so if you start with G  CP space and your topic of entrant  instance or interest so for instance GCP  instance billing will give you a bunch  of articles the top one or two links  will almost always be directly relevant  in exactly what you want if you're  trying to figure out how to use the  command line always start with g-cloud  compute resource so g-cloud compute  instances g-cloud compute instance  groups auto scalars whenever you want to  manage one of those pieces using the  command line where I as I was using the  UI you would look for the g-cloud  compute command and again it will give  you what you need the documentation is  actually pretty good typically when you  search you're going to end up in some  sort of a how-to guide or a QuickStart  but if you look at the breadcrumbs at  the top of the article you can actually  go up to the top where you'll see a  top-level table of contents and this is  relatively standard across all the  products so once you master this for GCE  for instance you can use it for App  Engine or Google Cloud storage or  whatever so there will be quick starts  and this is a very short exercise that  gets your hands dirty and gets you up  and running on the technology there are  how-to guides so this for instance would  have guides on how do you build an auto  scaler how do you create an image how do  you create an instance group how do you  create a load balancer and then there  are deep dives into api's  and concepts and so forth a few notes on  how to best evaluate how well compute  engine will serve your needs for  particular use cases in your environment  so the first thing to consider is when  you're trying to make a decision about  applying a new technology to business  opportunities you want to take it  seriously it's not hard but have a  process and follow the process  typically what we recommend to people is  that you start by defining some decision  criteria what is it that you need to  know in order to make a decision that  yes this technology will work for you so  for instance does it scale to meet our  company's usage needs is it easy to  configure can our existing systems  operational professionals get up to  speed within a couple of days once  you've decided what it is you need to  know then you figure out how to test so  you're going to take an existing use  case and generally you're going to  create a Minimum Viable Product that  emulates either something you're already  doing or something that you hope to do  and once you have figured out how to  test so what is it you're gonna build  then you do some research to figure out  exactly how to architect that on the  platform then you're gonna actually go  ahead and build it you also need to  decide who's gonna be on the team and  they're key players you need to have  somebody who's going to be responsible  for the architecture of the application  so for instance are you using cloud  sequel as part of the solution how are  you going to do load balancing where are  you going to locate servers you need  some operations people these are the  people who are gonna actually be  standing up the instances and managing  the configuration you need somebody  who's going to be responsible for  program managing and then reporting out  the results and then obviously you need  somebody in in the business side of  things to help with the decision-making  typically we find like one month per use  case is a pretty good time period so in  the first week you do your defining of  the criteria you recruit your team and  you agree on what you're gonna test in  weeks two and three you define your  architecture and you actually perform  some pre testing to make sure that you  understand how all the pieces work and  you're doing things right and then in  the last week we actually perform the  test consolidate the results and you  make a decision now obviously this  timeline will change depending on the  complexity of your use cases as well as  the investment that you've put in terms  of how many people are on the team this  could be longer or shorter but two to  three month trial period will give you  the opportunity to test a couple of  different use cases and make a really  intelligent decision of  how to apply GCE to your particular use  cases so hopefully that gives you a good  sense of how to go about evaluating  google compute engine for specific use  cases in your environment and that's all  for this session thanks for joining us  we'll be sending you an email that has a  link to code lab which allows you to try  out on your own some of the concepts  that we covered during this session we'd  also love to get your feedback so  provide that feedback on screen and stay  tuned for our next session
 

